# 1. ARCHITECTURAL STANDARDS (The "No Spaghetti" Rule)
- Always use a Layered Architecture (Routes -> Controllers -> Services).
- Backend: TypeScript, Express 4, MongoDB via Mongoose (server/).
- Frontend: React 19 (Vite), Redux Toolkit, Material UI (MUI), custom hooks for logic (client/).
- The client is mid-migration from .jsx to .ts/.tsx: new files are TypeScript.
- Folder Structure: keep logic in the existing 'services', 'hooks', 'types', and 'components' folders.

# 2. DATABASE & DATA INTEGRITY
- MongoDB runs as a replica set (rs0) because stock changes use transactions.
- Any operation that changes stock must run in a session/transaction together with its Transaction (audit) record.
- Validate all client input in the service layer (server/utils/validation.ts): ObjectIds, positive integer quantities,
  escaped search regexes, whitelisted sort fields.
- Database field names are camelCase.
- Avoid 'any'; define interfaces in the 'types' directories.

# 3. SECURITY
- Every route except login/register/setup is behind the `auth` middleware; role checks use `isAdmin` / `isManagerOrAdmin`.
- Roles: user (stock in/out, transfers), manager (+ catalog and users), admin (+ deletes, system operations).
- Never return password hashes (the User model hides `password` by default).
- Error responses always have the shape { success: false, message }.

# 4. DEVOPS & DEPLOYMENT
- docker-compose.yml runs the full stack locally; docker-compose.ims.yml is production (images from GHCR).
- Use .env files for sensitive data (see .env.example); never hardcode secrets.

# 5. CODING STYLE
- Use functional components in React.
- Use MUI icons (@mui/icons-material).
- When refactoring, use the 'Red/Green' diff view to ensure no logic is lost.
